WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.914 --> 00:00:06.580
LAKS LAKSHMANAN:
Welcome to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.560
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.050
We're hosting webinars
every Tuesday.

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:13.210
My name is Laks
Lakshmanan, and I'm

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.610
going to be talking about
a development workflow

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.000
when you're building end-to-end
machine learning models

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.190
with TensorFlow on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:24.970
You can ask questions anytime
and we have Googlers on standby

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:26.920
to answer them.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.960
So this talk that
I'm going to be doing

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.439
is basically going to be walking
through a set of code labs.

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:35.680
And the code lab is
here on that link,

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.415
and it's essentially about
building an end-to-end machine

00:00:38.415 --> 00:00:39.040
learning model.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.440
We'll walk through all of the
steps in order to do that.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.290
But before we do
that, let's talk

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:46.300
a little bit about what
machine learning looks

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.392
like on Google Cloud.

00:00:48.392 --> 00:00:49.850
So what we are
going to be doing is

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:53.470
that we're going to be using
distributed TensorFlow on Cloud

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:54.310
ML Engine.

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.980
And when you look at TensorFlow,
you have varying levels

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:00.940
of abstraction levels, with the
bottom level essentially being

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.019
how you run core
TensorFlow in C++.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:07.540
But more commonly, people do
core TensorFlow in Python,

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:12.160
which essentially calls the
C++ API to get high performance

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:13.690
on a variety of
different devices,

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:17.440
whether they are CPUs
or GPUs, et cetera.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.210
The level at which
we will be working at

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:20.740
will be tf.estimator.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:25.090
This is a very high level
out-of-the-box API that handles

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:28.370
distribution training for you.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:32.870
So why do we talk about
distributor TensorFlow?

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:36.100
When we look at a lot of
machine learning frameworks,

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.330
they can handle what
I call toy problems.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:42.260
Toy problems are problems where
the input fits into memory.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.660
Well, you take your data,
your inputs, your training,

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:46.730
your model, and you
have your model, right?

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:50.600
And if all you have are small
data sets, then that's fine,

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.670
and that's what I
call a toy problem.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:56.400
But in order to build
effective machine learning,

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.980
you need three things.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:02.150
And the first and foremost
thing that you need

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.160
is large amounts of data.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:08.090
And if you have big data, if
you have large amounts of data,

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:11.820
then batching and distribution
become extremely important.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.960
You need to take your inputs,
and you can't read all of them

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.820
from memory all at once.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:19.310
You have to break it up
into chunks and read them.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:24.980
You can't go ahead and train
your model on one CPU or GPU.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:28.850
You often have to distribute
to multiple compute nodes.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:31.744
And once you have batching
and you have distribution--

00:02:31.744 --> 00:02:33.410
and this is something
that you need when

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.300
you have large data sizes--

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:40.330
then TensorFlow starts
to play a huge role.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:41.767
The other thing
that you need when

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:43.600
you want to build
effective machine learning

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.270
is that you need what's
called feature engineering.

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:49.270
This is where you don't
use the raw inputs as is,

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:52.930
but you take your raw inputs and
you do transformations of them.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:54.610
You may preprocess those inputs.

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.080
You may create features.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.880
And we look at
examples of those,

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:00.590
and it's on those features
that you train your model.

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:03.470
And the reason to do this
is that the raw data,

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:05.920
it can be pretty noisy.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.440
And it can be
relatively hard and take

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.830
many, many, many epochs and
a much more complex model

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:14.440
for the model to learn
from the raw data, where

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.990
if you've done
feature engineering,

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:20.669
it might be easier for
your model to learn.

00:03:20.669 --> 00:03:22.210
So the third thing
that you need when

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:24.043
you want to build
effective machine learning

00:03:24.043 --> 00:03:26.290
is that you need to
be able to experiment

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:29.410
with a variety of different
model architectures.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:33.490
And that's another place where
TensorFlow comes in extremely

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:38.710
handy, because it is the most
popular tool for deep learning,

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:43.660
and importantly, it's very
popular, both among researchers

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:44.987
and among developers.

00:03:44.987 --> 00:03:46.570
So what that means
is that when you're

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:50.710
developing with TensorFlow,
you get the benefit

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:53.200
of, essentially,
being able to build

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:57.250
models that are leading edge.

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:01.000
And you get to do this often
by basically taking open source

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:04.710
code and applying
it to your data.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:07.970
So that's essentially
what TensorFlow provides.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.310
But what else does a
machine learning framework

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:12.740
need to provide,
besides the ability

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.950
to handle large data, the
ability to feature engineering,

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:19.670
and the ability to experiment
and model architectures?

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:22.160
What else do we need?

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:27.950
Well, one thing is
hyperparameter tuning.

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:30.520
Sure, you can train a
model, but oftentimes,

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:34.240
you don't train a model, you
train a family of models.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.520
And you want to
basically go ahead and do

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:40.450
this experimentation
easily, in an automated way,

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:43.510
and that's where
hyperparameter tuning comes in.

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:45.310
Another thing that
you may want to do

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:47.320
is that once you
have your model,

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.600
you have clients who are
accessing this model.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.570
And you need to basically
auto scale the prediction code

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:55.350
so that if you have 100
clients or a million clients,

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.850
your prediction API scales.

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:00.100
And so you want your
client code to be

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:03.460
able to access your
model not through Python,

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:06.940
not through TensorFlow, but
with just a REST API call,

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:10.210
so that you basically are
able to access this prediction

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:13.780
model from pretty much
any device, any language

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:16.590
that you want.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:19.260
But then the question becomes,
if the client is basically

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:21.510
going to be making
REST API calls,

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:24.000
who's going to do all of the
preprocessing and feature

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:24.810
creation?

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.330
Because your model, the
model that you've deployed,

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.877
expects to see
these new features.

00:05:29.877 --> 00:05:30.960
So who's going to do that?

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.290
Because what the client wants
to give is the raw data.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:37.740
And that's where Cloud
ML Engine comes in.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:42.330
Cloud ML Engine helps you
to basically do auto tuning,

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.550
helps you to scale
out your predictions,

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.400
and helps you maintain
it in such a way

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.850
that you get repeatable
results between training

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:53.530
and between prediction.

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:56.280
So the way we go
through the development

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:59.940
workflow in ML Engine is
that you start with Datalab.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:02.940
And Datalab is in Jupyter
Notebook that's hosted.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:04.845
So you basically
start with Datalab,

00:06:04.845 --> 00:06:06.510
which is in Jupyter Notebook.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:08.100
And you do it on
a small data set.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.840
Maybe you have a CSV file.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.300
You're doing your
exploration in pandas.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:14.520
You may be using Apache
Beam, and then you're

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:15.780
writing a TensorFlow model.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:16.680
You're improving it.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.709
And then you get a
model that works.

00:06:18.709 --> 00:06:20.250
And once you have
a model that works,

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:21.790
you don't know
how well it works.

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:24.120
And the way you basically
figure out how well it works

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:26.490
is that you take it
now and you scale

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.890
it out to your entire data set.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.920
And you do that on Google
Cloud, where you're basically

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:36.690
using things like Cloud data
to do your preprocessing.

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:39.240
You're reading your
data off Cloud storage,

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:43.050
and you're running your model on
many machines using ML Engine.

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:46.470
And that helps you, on all of
these things, are serverless.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:48.270
You don't have to
manage any clusters.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.260
You don't have to manage
any infrastructure.

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:53.700
So that's basically
the end-to-end workflow

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.260
that I'm going to
be walking through.

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:58.270
So we're going to start
with how to take your data,

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:00.640
explore it, visualize
it, and then

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:03.990
to create a smaller part of
that data to play around with,

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.330
to create your sample
data set, and then

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:09.450
to be able to develop your
first TensorFlow model.

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:10.980
And having gotten
your model, you

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:13.410
want to now try it out
on the entire data set.

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:16.590
So you go back and you create
your training and evaluation

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:19.950
data sets that are large,
that are over the entire data.

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:22.860
And having done that, you
execute the training--

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.360
this model that you
have developed--

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:27.120
you execute the
training, you deploy it,

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.870
and then you basically
access it from other clients,

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:32.890
for example, from
a web application.

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:36.255
So these are the seven
kind of steps in this.

00:07:36.255 --> 00:07:40.030
And this link takes
you to the code lab

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:41.950
and to the GitHub repository.

00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:43.900
So here is the code
lab, and here's

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:45.820
a GitHub repository
that contains

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:46.870
all of these notebooks.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:48.280
Or that's notebook 1.

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:51.815
That's notebook 2, notebook
3, et cetera, all the way.

00:07:51.815 --> 00:07:54.190
So we'll basically go ahead
and walk through each of them

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:55.430
one by one.

00:07:55.430 --> 00:08:00.000
So the first thing is
to explore the data.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.890
Now, the model that
I'm going to be doing

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:04.695
is going to be a
structured data model.

00:08:04.695 --> 00:08:06.070
And you might say,
wait a second.

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:07.360
You're doing structured data.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:08.770
Isn't that boring?

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:11.050
Well, it's kind of boring, yes.

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:13.662
Images and sequences
get all the press.

00:08:13.662 --> 00:08:16.120
But even at Google, where we've
been doing machine learning

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:20.109
for a long time, MLP,
multilayer perceptrons--

00:08:20.109 --> 00:08:22.150
which are basically the
kind of model that we use

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:23.470
for structured data--

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:25.810
that is nearly 2/3
of all the models

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:27.310
that we have in production.

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:30.490
LSTMs, which are sequence
models, the kinds of things

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:34.450
that you do for time series and
for text, they're another 30%.

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:38.289
The Hello World, [INAUDIBLE]
of the world, the images,

00:08:38.289 --> 00:08:40.645
that's less than 5%
of production models.

00:08:40.645 --> 00:08:42.520
So if you're starting
out in machine learning

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:45.490
and you're saying, what
should I concentrate on?

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:47.810
Where do you start is
with structured data,

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:50.340
so that's where
we're going to start.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:52.700
So our goal in here is
that we want to predict

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:53.870
the weight of a newborn.

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:55.700
So this is the
health care data set.

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:57.860
And the idea is that
if you can predict

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:00.170
the weight of a
newborn, we can identify

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:03.800
babies that might need special
facilities, incubators,

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:06.930
et cetera, and then get babies
the care that they need.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:10.430
So the idea is that a pregnant
woman walks into a doctor's

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:13.850
office, and based on a
variety of measurements

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:16.160
that the doctor
has, they want to be

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:18.155
able to predict what the
weight of that newborn

00:09:18.155 --> 00:09:20.150
is, which is a
pretty good indicator

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:22.650
of the health of the baby.

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:23.920
So how do we do that?

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:26.745
We need a data set, and the
data set that we're going to use

00:09:26.745 --> 00:09:29.880
is an open data set collected
by the US government.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:33.150
It's all the births recorded
in the 50 states in the US.

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.280
So this is called a
natality data set.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.150
It's about 137 million rows,
pretty reasonable sized data

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:41.760
set that we can use to
demonstrate the kinds of things

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:43.980
that we want to do.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:49.540
So this data set includes
things like the birth,

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:51.860
the date of birth, the
location of the birth.

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:54.331
Also, the baby's birth
weight in pounds.

00:09:54.331 --> 00:09:55.580
This is going to be our label.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:57.538
This is the thing that
we're trying to predict.

00:09:57.538 --> 00:10:00.830
And things like the mother's
age at the time of birth,

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:03.110
the gestation weeks, how
long was the pregnancy?

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:05.120
Was it a preemie pregnancy?

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:07.212
Was it full term, et cetera.

00:10:07.212 --> 00:10:09.170
So those are the kinds
of features that we have

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:14.150
that we can use as predictors to
predict the weight of the baby.

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:18.030
So first thing, to explore
and visualize the data set.

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:20.300
So if you go to the
code lab link there,

00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:22.910
so number one is to
explore the data.

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:26.960
So we basically are
going to go ahead and go

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:30.110
into the Cloud Shell icon.

00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:34.170
So I already have that
running here, so there it is.

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:36.590
Here's my Cloud Shell.

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:37.340
OK.

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:40.160
And in here, I can
go into Cloud Shell.

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:46.880
I can then basically run the
first command, G-Cloud Compute

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:51.410
Zones list, which is here.

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:52.840
So go down there.

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:54.600
G-Cloud Compute Zones list.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.630
This tells me all the compute
zones that are available,

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:59.670
and you want to go ahead
and pick a zone that's

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:02.880
close by to where you live
to reduce your latency

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:05.310
and to basically
have your compute

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:07.410
in that particular region.

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:12.450
The next step is to basically go
ahead and create a Datalab VM.

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:14.980
Just to save time,
I've already done this,

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:16.500
so I'll skip that step.

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:20.010
And the third thing is to go
into the BigQuery console.

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:22.500
So we can go to the
BigQuery console

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:31.500
by going into here,
into the GCP console.

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:33.960
Going down, let's go
into the BigQuery.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:35.790
There is BigQuery.

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:40.490
And once we are in
BigQuery, we want

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:43.940
to go ahead and compose a query.

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:48.170
And the query that we
want to compose is this.

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:55.180
And we want to do
this in standard SQL.

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:56.990
So I'll just go ahead
and do standard SQL.

00:11:56.990 --> 00:11:59.680
Another way to do this is
to go into Show Options

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:02.950
and turn off the
Legacy SQL query.

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:04.960
So let's go ahead and
do the standard SQL.

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:06.920
That is the query
that I want to run.

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:09.940
The query is that I'm going
into this data set called

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:11.980
Public Samples Natality.

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:13.950
And I'm basically
picking up all the births

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:16.450
that are between 2000 and 2005.

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:17.920
Let me go ahead
and run the query.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:21.340
And I'm basically going ahead
and finding the plurality.

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:24.160
The plurality is
whether a single child

