00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:15.230
So one of the things that
I kind of glossed over

00:23:15.230 --> 00:23:18.410
as I went through was that
the way I split my data

00:23:18.410 --> 00:23:20.330
was that I didn't use rand.

00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:23.030
Instead, what I did
was that I used a hash.

00:23:23.030 --> 00:23:26.440
And the reason to use a hash
rather than a rand is that

00:23:26.440 --> 00:23:28.940
in this data set-- and this is
going to be common with a lot

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:30.070
of different data sets--

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:32.690
is that the rows
are not actually

00:23:32.690 --> 00:23:34.340
independent of each other.

00:23:34.340 --> 00:23:37.040
If you have triplets,
you have three babies.

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:38.960
So you have three
rows in your data,

00:23:38.960 --> 00:23:41.460
and those three rows
are for the same month.

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:43.520
So it's the same number
of gestation weeks,

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:46.320
the same mother's age,
the same everything.

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:48.380
So they are not really
independent rows.

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:51.220
And you don't want to
actually put those rows,

00:23:51.220 --> 00:23:52.790
like one of those
rows in training

00:23:52.790 --> 00:23:55.890
and another of the
rows in evaluation.

00:23:55.890 --> 00:23:58.520
So what we do is that we
make a hash of the row

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:02.160
and we divide based on the hash.

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:04.700
So we've now created
our sample data set.

00:24:04.700 --> 00:24:07.070
The next step is
to build the model.

00:24:07.070 --> 00:24:09.494
To build the model,
the thing to realize

00:24:09.494 --> 00:24:11.660
is that there are two types
of features in our data.

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:14.180
We have dense features and
we have sparse features.

00:24:14.180 --> 00:24:16.730
Dense features are
features like the price,

00:24:16.730 --> 00:24:18.260
which is a continuous number.

00:24:18.260 --> 00:24:20.390
And sparse feature is
a categorical feature,

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:23.750
like an employee ID, or
the gender of the baby.

00:24:23.750 --> 00:24:26.420
Is it male, is it
female, or is it unknown?

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:29.400
That's a categorical variable.

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:32.360
So you have dense variables
and you have sparse variables.

00:24:32.360 --> 00:24:34.850
And deep neural
networks are really

00:24:34.850 --> 00:24:37.490
good for dense,
highly correlated data

00:24:37.490 --> 00:24:40.670
because of the way they work
with adding and subtracting

00:24:40.670 --> 00:24:42.170
data between layers.

00:24:42.170 --> 00:24:44.690
But on the other
hand, sparse networks

00:24:44.690 --> 00:24:48.197
are really well-suited
to linear models.

00:24:48.197 --> 00:24:50.780
So if you have sparse features,
you want to use linear models.

00:24:50.780 --> 00:24:52.310
If you have dense
features, you want

00:24:52.310 --> 00:24:54.350
to use deep neural networks.

00:24:54.350 --> 00:24:56.300
But in this case, we have both.

00:24:56.300 --> 00:24:57.450
So what do we do?

00:24:57.450 --> 00:25:00.290
We use what's called a
wide and deep model, where

00:25:00.290 --> 00:25:02.030
we take all of our
sparse features

00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:04.400
and we put them into the
wide part of the network,

00:25:04.400 --> 00:25:06.230
and we take all of
our dense features

00:25:06.230 --> 00:25:08.420
and we put them in the
deep part of the network.

00:25:08.420 --> 00:25:11.120
And the way you do
this in TensorFlow

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:14.150
is to use what is called a DNN
Linear Combined Classifier.

00:25:14.150 --> 00:25:15.650
So we basically are
going to do this

00:25:15.650 --> 00:25:18.450
and we're going to take some
of our features and say,

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:21.930
these are our linear future
and these are our DNN features.

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:23.666
So let's go ahead and
take a quick look.

00:25:23.666 --> 00:25:25.040
And in this case,
what I'll do is

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:29.580
I'll just step through
the code on GitHub itself.

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:30.830
But you can basically do this.

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:33.690
You can run the code and
you can train the model.

00:25:33.690 --> 00:25:37.160
So here, as before, you change
your bucket, your project.

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:39.940
And having done that,
here's a TensorFlow code.

00:25:39.940 --> 00:25:41.810
We import TensorFlow.

00:25:41.810 --> 00:25:45.110
We say my data set contains
the CSV columns, the weight

00:25:45.110 --> 00:25:49.340
in pounds, is the baby male,
the mother's age, the plurality,

00:25:49.340 --> 00:25:51.260
and the gestation weeks.

00:25:51.260 --> 00:25:54.770
And what you want to predict
is the weight in pounds.

00:25:54.770 --> 00:25:58.280
And having done that,
to read the data set,

00:25:58.280 --> 00:26:01.250
I'm going to basically go
ahead and glob all file names.

00:26:01.250 --> 00:26:06.620
Globbing is basically putting in
a wild star, like an asterisk.

00:26:06.620 --> 00:26:09.020
So basically go
ahead and reading

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:11.300
all the files that have
a particular prefix.

00:26:11.300 --> 00:26:13.640
Because in reality, it will
not be reading one file,

00:26:13.640 --> 00:26:16.400
it will be reading a
set of sharded files.

00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:18.680
So I'll be able
to say train star.

00:26:18.680 --> 00:26:21.980
So having done that, I say
this is a text data set.

00:26:21.980 --> 00:26:24.730
Read it line by line
and decode the CSV.

00:26:24.730 --> 00:26:26.690
How do I decode the CSV?

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:29.930
I take the CSV, and
these are my columns.

00:26:29.930 --> 00:26:31.460
So basically, those
are my defaults.

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:32.660
That's my columns.

00:26:32.660 --> 00:26:34.670
So for each of them,
take out the label,

00:26:34.670 --> 00:26:36.480
because that's not a feature.

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:39.170
So you have your features,
which are one set of columns.

00:26:39.170 --> 00:26:40.670
And you have the
label column, which

00:26:40.670 --> 00:26:43.220
is this one column,
the original data set.

00:26:43.220 --> 00:26:46.370
So having done that, then go
ahead and shuffle the data.

00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:48.830
Shuffling is a good thing to
do in distributed training

00:26:48.830 --> 00:26:51.720
so that each worker gets a
different copy of the data,

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:54.710
in essence, or is processing
a different slice of the data

00:26:54.710 --> 00:26:56.010
at the same time.

00:26:56.010 --> 00:26:59.510
And you basically go ahead and
repeat the data as many times

00:26:59.510 --> 00:27:02.720
as you need, and that's
how you read the data.

00:27:02.720 --> 00:27:06.290
And now, we basically
say, given our data,

00:27:06.290 --> 00:27:09.270
how do we form our wide
columns or our deep columns?

00:27:09.270 --> 00:27:11.390
Well, we basically
say that the is

00:27:11.390 --> 00:27:14.810
male is a categorical column.

00:27:14.810 --> 00:27:17.660
Mother's age is
a numeric column.

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:20.180
Plurality is a
categorical column,

00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:21.740
and this is the vocabulary.

00:27:21.740 --> 00:27:24.690
And the gestation weeks
is a numeric column.

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:28.100
So you basically specify
what type of column it is,

00:27:28.100 --> 00:27:30.494
and that's our input data.

00:27:30.494 --> 00:27:31.910
And what I'm now
doing is that I'm

00:27:31.910 --> 00:27:35.404
going to take my common
columns, my numeric columns,

00:27:35.404 --> 00:27:36.820
and do what is
called bucketizing.

00:27:36.820 --> 00:27:40.216
I'm going to discretize them,
to basically put them into bins.

00:27:40.216 --> 00:27:41.840
And in this case,
I'm just putting them

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:44.625
into bins of 15 to
45 in steps of one.

00:27:44.625 --> 00:27:46.250
And then I'm going
to do the same thing

00:27:46.250 --> 00:27:47.900
for the gestation weeks.

00:27:47.900 --> 00:27:52.280
And now, my wide columns are
the is male and the plurality--

00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:53.540
those are both sparse--

00:27:53.540 --> 00:27:57.230
but also the discretized
numeric columns.

00:27:57.230 --> 00:27:59.780
And having done that-- now this
is kind of where TensorFlow

00:27:59.780 --> 00:28:01.379
comes in really
handy, is that it

00:28:01.379 --> 00:28:03.170
can do all these very
sophisticated things.

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:05.360
So sure, I've discretized
it, but now I'm

00:28:05.360 --> 00:28:07.780
going to basically go ahead
and do my feature crossing.

00:28:07.780 --> 00:28:11.060
And I'm going to feature cross
these extremely sparse data

00:28:11.060 --> 00:28:14.240
to basically create,
in effect, something

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:16.850
like 20,000 new columns
of feature crossing.

00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:20.000
I can afford to do this because
my data set is very large.

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:23.240
So having done that, I then
do dimensional reduction

00:28:23.240 --> 00:28:24.410
to a set of three.

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:26.900
Now, the question is, do
you need to do all this?

00:28:26.900 --> 00:28:29.000
If I didn't do any
of these things,

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:32.960
if I just took my raw data and
I did it, I would lose about 30%

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:35.630
in [? RMSE. ?] So I get
about 30% improvement

00:28:35.630 --> 00:28:37.490
by doing some of these
sophisticated feature

00:28:37.490 --> 00:28:39.530
crosses, et cetera.

00:28:39.530 --> 00:28:41.750
And then I basically
create my deep column

00:28:41.750 --> 00:28:43.640
so I have my wide and deep.

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:46.490
And then I can
train and evaluate.

00:28:46.490 --> 00:28:48.980
By creating a DNN
Linear Regressor

00:28:48.980 --> 00:28:51.860
with the wide columns,
with the deep columns,

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:55.850
and then saying what it is
I need to do for training.

00:28:55.850 --> 00:28:57.830
I'm going to retrain that CSV.

00:28:57.830 --> 00:28:59.880
What do I need to do
for my evaluation?

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:02.000
Evaluate on eval.csv.

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:03.420
So that's pretty much it.

00:29:03.420 --> 00:29:04.700
And I do one thing else.

00:29:04.700 --> 00:29:06.908
Now, this is something that
a lot of people don't do,

00:29:06.908 --> 00:29:08.496
but a very good thing
to do, something

00:29:08.496 --> 00:29:10.370
that you want to get
into the habit of doing,

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:13.200
because it's going to be useful
for every step after this,

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:15.830
is that I also export the model.

00:29:15.830 --> 00:29:19.310
The reason to export a model
is to basically take my model

00:29:19.310 --> 00:29:21.865
that I've trained and
put it into a form

00:29:21.865 --> 00:29:26.390
where it can be quickly wrapped
up and used for production.

00:29:26.390 --> 00:29:28.400
So I basically export my model.

00:29:28.400 --> 00:29:30.614
And when you're
exporting a model,

00:29:30.614 --> 00:29:32.030
you need to think
about-- remember

00:29:32.030 --> 00:29:35.240
I talked about the REST
API by which you access it?

00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:37.990
So here's the things
that someone is going

00:29:37.990 --> 00:29:40.730
to give us in that REST API.

00:29:40.730 --> 00:29:44.210
They're going to give us the is
male, mother's age, plurality,

00:29:44.210 --> 00:29:45.440
and gestation weeks.

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:47.300
What does this look like?

00:29:47.300 --> 00:29:49.730
It basically looks
something like this.

00:29:49.730 --> 00:29:53.330
Somebody would be able
to go into and create

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:55.940
an application that
looks like this, where

00:29:55.940 --> 00:29:59.930
that's the mother's age, this is
the number of gestation weeks.

00:29:59.930 --> 00:30:02.150
And I expect that it is twins.

00:30:02.150 --> 00:30:03.470
Both of them are male.

00:30:03.470 --> 00:30:05.160
And go ahead and predict.

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:12.290
So that's the idea that we
want to do when we're basically

00:30:12.290 --> 00:30:14.930
creating a TensorFlow model.

00:30:14.930 --> 00:30:18.110
So having done
that, the next step

00:30:18.110 --> 00:30:21.290
is to preprocess the
data with Dataflow.

00:30:21.290 --> 00:30:23.120
So we've created a
TensorFlow model.

00:30:23.120 --> 00:30:24.440
It's being developed.

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:26.997
The next step is to
operationalize the model,

00:30:26.997 --> 00:30:28.830
is to take that model
and operationalize it.

00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:31.580
Now, this model we
created is ready to go,

00:30:31.580 --> 00:30:34.160
except we don't have
the full data yet.

00:30:34.160 --> 00:30:37.310
Now, the way we created
the small data set before

00:30:37.310 --> 00:30:38.910
was to use pandas.

00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:41.270
pandas is fine
for experimenting,

00:30:41.270 --> 00:30:43.290
but if you want to
operationalize you workflow,

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:44.581
you want to make it repeatable.

00:30:44.581 --> 00:30:47.330
You want to have it deal
with streaming data.

00:30:47.330 --> 00:30:50.090
You want to do your
preprocessing not in pandas,

00:30:50.090 --> 00:30:51.740
but in something
that's very scalable,

00:30:51.740 --> 00:30:53.270
something like Apache Beam.

00:30:53.270 --> 00:30:56.600
So that is going
to be our next lab.

00:30:56.600 --> 00:30:59.840
So the idea is to basically
write this Apache Beam pipeline

00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:02.420
and lob it off to
Cloud Dataflow,

00:31:02.420 --> 00:31:04.490
to the servers that
will run this for us.

00:31:04.490 --> 00:31:07.100
It will auto scale all
of these predictions,

00:31:07.100 --> 00:31:09.650
and it'll basically give us
a number of things that are

00:31:09.650 --> 00:31:15.050
[? run. ?] So we are now
here onto the next notebook,

00:31:15.050 --> 00:31:16.370
which is on preprocessing.

00:31:16.370 --> 00:31:19.880
And I'll skip to the key
part of this notebook.

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:21.350
And the key part
of this notebook

00:31:21.350 --> 00:31:23.420
is that we are
reading from BigQuery.

00:31:23.420 --> 00:31:25.880
We are reading the
data from BigQuery,

00:31:25.880 --> 00:31:29.210
and we are doing the same
things that we did in pandas,

00:31:29.210 --> 00:31:32.570
except that we are now
doing it in Apache Beam

00:31:32.570 --> 00:31:35.400
so that it can also
work on streaming data.

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:38.750
So imagine that you have
data about births coming in

00:31:38.750 --> 00:31:40.660
from all the clinics
in the country,

00:31:40.660 --> 00:31:43.470
as they are streaming into
[INAUDIBLE] you can read them.

00:31:43.470 --> 00:31:46.100
You can do this preprocessing,
and then you can stream them

00:31:46.100 --> 00:31:47.630
out of your database.

00:31:47.630 --> 00:31:50.100
So we are basically creating
a no ultrasound and a

00:31:50.100 --> 00:31:51.630
with ultrasound.

00:31:51.630 --> 00:31:54.650
And if it's no ultrasound,
the gender is unknown.

00:31:54.650 --> 00:31:56.300
And then we are
basically going ahead,

00:31:56.300 --> 00:32:00.200
and if the plurality is
greater than 1, it is multiple.

00:32:00.200 --> 00:32:05.780
And also, when I go
ahead and do the query,

00:32:05.780 --> 00:32:08.750
I do the filtering,
is a weight in pounds

00:32:08.750 --> 00:32:11.160
greater than 0, et cetera.

00:32:11.160 --> 00:32:13.250
So we basically go
ahead and do this.

00:32:13.250 --> 00:32:15.530
And we lob it off.

00:32:15.530 --> 00:32:17.460
Now, once you run
this notebook, it

00:32:17.460 --> 00:32:20.130
will lob off the
code into Dataflow.

00:32:20.130 --> 00:32:22.160
A few minutes later,
you will basically

00:32:22.160 --> 00:32:26.900
have your large data set
that's been created on,

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:29.090
in this case, five nodes.

00:32:29.090 --> 00:32:31.190
In this case,
actually, 15 nodes.

00:32:31.190 --> 00:32:32.549
The current [? CPU ?] is 15.

00:32:32.549 --> 00:32:34.340
So it just basically
goes ahead and does it

00:32:34.340 --> 00:32:38.120
on however many
workers you specify.

00:32:38.120 --> 00:32:42.640
And having done that, the next
step is to train on ML Engine.

00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:44.840
Again, next notebook.

00:32:44.840 --> 00:32:47.810
And the key point here,
in terms of training,

00:32:47.810 --> 00:32:50.310
is very, very, very
straightforward.

00:32:50.310 --> 00:32:51.650
You have your code.

00:32:51.650 --> 00:32:55.010
You basically tell ML
Engine, here's my code.

00:32:55.010 --> 00:32:57.020
It exists in this
Python package.

00:32:57.020 --> 00:32:58.760
Please train it for me.

00:32:58.760 --> 00:32:59.910
That's pretty much it.

00:32:59.910 --> 00:33:01.460
You run a single command.

00:33:01.460 --> 00:33:06.020
It basically lobs it off
and it creates the machines.

00:33:06.020 --> 00:33:09.080
If it's on a GPU, it
basically attaches GPUs

00:33:09.080 --> 00:33:10.160
to those machines.

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:12.470
It does the training,
and the trained model

00:33:12.470 --> 00:33:14.270
shows up on Cloud Storage.

00:33:14.270 --> 00:33:16.646
And once a trained model
is on Cloud Storage,

00:33:16.646 --> 00:33:18.020
even while it's
training, you can

00:33:18.020 --> 00:33:20.690
do your monitoring, et cetera.

00:33:20.690 --> 00:33:22.310
Then you deploy it.

00:33:22.310 --> 00:33:24.620
Deploying, again, last notebook.

00:33:24.620 --> 00:33:28.940
To deploy it, it's, again, just
a single command line call.

00:33:28.940 --> 00:33:31.370
Again, once you write
a TensorFlow model,

00:33:31.370 --> 00:33:34.820
everything after that is
essentially just single command

00:33:34.820 --> 00:33:35.720
line calls.

00:33:35.720 --> 00:33:38.180
You basically say,
G-Cloud ML Engine, please

00:33:38.180 --> 00:33:41.810
create a version of this
model from this TensorFlow.

00:33:41.810 --> 00:33:44.420
And then once that's
deployed, any time it

00:33:44.420 --> 00:33:47.750
receives a JSON of this
form, it basically goes ahead

00:33:47.750 --> 00:33:49.610
and sends back a
prediction, which

00:33:49.610 --> 00:33:52.910
is why something
like this just works.

00:33:52.910 --> 00:33:54.620
So we can say the
mother's age is 30.

00:33:54.620 --> 00:33:56.300
There's 6.2 pounds.

00:33:56.300 --> 00:33:58.580
If it's a single child,
what is the prediction that

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:00.692
7.9 pounds, et cetera.

00:34:00.692 --> 00:34:01.400
Boom, boom, boom.

00:34:01.400 --> 00:34:03.290
You're basically
getting predictions back

00:34:03.290 --> 00:34:06.390
because you've
essentially deployed it.

00:34:06.390 --> 00:34:09.739
Then that's pretty much
the end-to-end process

00:34:09.739 --> 00:34:11.870
of going ahead and
building a model.

00:34:11.870 --> 00:34:13.880
So bottom line,
explore your data.

00:34:13.880 --> 00:34:15.290
Create your sample data set.

00:34:15.290 --> 00:34:17.000
Create a TensorFlow model.

00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:22.699
And then operationalize it using
Dataflow and using ML Engine.

00:34:22.699 --> 00:34:24.210
And I'll skip this part.

00:34:24.210 --> 00:34:26.880
That's just building
a web application.

00:34:26.880 --> 00:34:31.354
And with that, just stay
tuned for a live Q&amp;A,

00:34:31.354 --> 00:34:33.020
and we'll be back in
less than a minute.

00:35:37.520 --> 00:35:41.830
OK, let's go to questions.

00:35:41.830 --> 00:35:42.550
All right.

00:35:42.550 --> 00:35:44.360
OK, so questions here.

00:35:44.360 --> 00:35:46.630
First question, is
there a way to do this

00:35:46.630 --> 00:35:48.400
without using Datalab?

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:50.530
Can I use my own Jupyter build?

00:35:50.530 --> 00:35:51.800
Absolutely.

00:35:51.800 --> 00:35:55.900
So Datalab is actually
an open source thing.

00:35:55.900 --> 00:35:59.160
So let's go ahead and
do GitHub and search

00:35:59.160 --> 00:36:03.240
for pydatalab, which is
the project that's in.

00:36:03.240 --> 00:36:05.490
And pydatalab is a package.

00:36:05.490 --> 00:36:07.080
So you can basically
go ahead and do

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:11.080
a pip install of
pydatalab within Jupyter,

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:13.080
and then you will have
all of these capabilities

00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:15.680
that I talked about, all of
the connections to BigQuery,

00:36:15.680 --> 00:36:17.680
and ML Engine, et cetera.

00:36:17.680 --> 00:36:19.860
So that's all you really need.

00:36:19.860 --> 00:36:22.530
So go into the Google
Datalab project.

00:36:22.530 --> 00:36:25.350
Datalab is the full
notebook itself,

00:36:25.350 --> 00:36:29.130
but pydatalab is the library
that this thing uses,

00:36:29.130 --> 00:36:31.080
and this is the
pip-installable package.

00:36:31.080 --> 00:36:34.200
So you can go ahead and
install it from within Jupyter.

00:36:34.200 --> 00:36:35.850
So if you're using
your own Jupyter

00:36:35.850 --> 00:36:38.130
build, no problem at all.

00:36:38.130 --> 00:36:40.560
You can still do all this.

00:36:40.560 --> 00:36:42.480
Next question.

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:45.450
Why did you use Cloud Dataflow
to do the preprocessing?

00:36:45.450 --> 00:36:46.620
What are my other options?

00:36:49.230 --> 00:36:51.300
In particular, can I use
something like Spark?

00:36:51.300 --> 00:36:52.830
Absolutely.

00:36:52.830 --> 00:36:56.310
The reason I use Cloud
Dataflow is because I always

00:36:56.310 --> 00:36:58.530
think about streaming,
and streaming to me

00:36:58.530 --> 00:37:01.877
is the limit of where
things are going to go.

00:37:01.877 --> 00:37:03.960
Anything that you're doing
in batch today-- people

00:37:03.960 --> 00:37:07.200
want to do things faster and
faster, and the limit of that

00:37:07.200 --> 00:37:09.930
is that you want to do
it as the data comes in.

00:37:09.930 --> 00:37:12.840
And the nice thing about
doing it in Dataflow

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:16.350
is that it's the same code
that works on both batch data,

00:37:16.350 --> 00:37:18.990
historical data, and
newly arriving data,

00:37:18.990 --> 00:37:20.370
or streaming data.

00:37:20.370 --> 00:37:22.080
But you can do this in Spark.

00:37:22.080 --> 00:37:23.580
The only problem
is that you will

